<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Treaty of San Ildefonso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Treaty of San Ildefonso was a secret agreement signed on 1 October 1800 by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In exchange <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> promised a kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, for the son-in-law of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> of Spain. Spain kept administering the colony until 1803, when France, after briefly taking possession, sold Louisiana to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, despite having promised Spain never to cede it to a third power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-treaty-of-san-ildefonso.jpg" alt="Third Treaty of San Ildefonso" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Louisiana_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had fought the French Republic after the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> in 1793, but was defeated in the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and made the Peace of Basel in 1795. The Second Treaty of San Ildefonso of 1796 then allied Spain with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which cost it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>, while the British blockade hurt an economy dependent on colonial trade; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-debt">national debt</a> rose eightfold between 1793 and 1798. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, received from France under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763, was costly to defend against American settlers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, having lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, Martinique and Guadeloupe between 1791 and 1794, sought new colonies. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-18-brumaire">Coup of 18 Brumaire</a> in November 1799, he and Charles Talleyrand hoped to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> supplying raw materials for French plantations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The French general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</a> negotiated with the former Spanish chief minister Mariano Luis de Urquijo. Berthier also asked for East and West Florida and ten warships; Urquijo refused the Floridas but agreed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> and six ships of the line of seventy-four guns. In return <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> wanted a state for his son-in-law Louis, Duke of Parma, whose inheritance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> meant to annex. The treaty named it only vaguely, perhaps Tuscany or the three Roman legations, and Urquijo held back Louisiana and the ships until France confirmed the territory. The treaty also reaffirmed the alliance of 1796.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> of 9 February 1801, the Treaty of Aranjuez of March 1801 confirmed the terms and created the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>. Spain's chief minister Manuel Godoy was attacked for terms seen as favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, though modern historians are less critical, since Spain effectively controlled only a small part of the territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> expedition failed, its leader General Charles Leclerc dying of yellow fever with an estimated 29,000 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to the U.S. in April 1803 for $15 million. Etruria was absorbed into France in 1807.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Treaty of San Ildefonso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Treaty of San Ildefonso</b> in 1800 Spain secretly returned Louisiana to France.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-louisiana">Spanish Louisiana</a></li></ul></section>
